Implement a support ticket classifier in a .NET 10 Web API project at `/tmp/sv-3e6c86271917471b9894d161372ec85d/TicketClassifier`.

## Current state
- `TicketClassifier.csproj` targets `net10.0`, has no packages beyond the web SDK
- `Program.cs` is minimal: builder + controllers + run
- `Data/tickets.csv` has columns: Subject, Description, Priority, Category (values: Billing, Technical, General) with 6 rows

## Requirements
Use **ML.NET** (`Microsoft.ML`) for multiclass classification. This is structured tabular data — do NOT use an LLM.

### 1. Add NuGet packages
Add `Microsoft.ML` (version 4.*) and `Microsoft.Extensions.ML` (version 4.*) to the csproj.

### 2. Create `Models/TicketData.cs`
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML.Data;

namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketData
{
    [LoadColumn(0)]
    public string Subject { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [LoadColumn(1)]
    public string Description { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [LoadColumn(2)]
    public float Priority { get; set; }

    [LoadColumn(3)]
    public string Category { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}

public class TicketPrediction
{
    [ColumnName("PredictedLabel")]
    public string PredictedCategory { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    public float[] Score { get; set; } = [];
}
```

### 3. Create `Services/ModelTrainerService.cs`
This service trains the model on startup and saves it to `Data/model.zip`. It should:
- Use `MLContext(seed: 42)` for reproducibility
- Combine Subject + Description into a single text feature using `CustomMapping`
- Build a pipeline: concatenate text → featurize text → append Priority → map label to key → train with `SdcaMaximumEntropy` → map key back to label
- Use `TrainTestSplit(testFraction: 0.2)` for evaluation
- Log multiclass classification metrics (LogLoss, MacroAccuracy, MicroAccuracy, per-class metrics) to console
- Save the trained model to `Data/model.zip`

Implementation:
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML;
using Microsoft.ML.Data;
using TicketClassifier.Models;

namespace TicketClassifier.Services;

public class ModelTrainerService
{
    public static void TrainAndSaveModel(string dataPath, string modelPath)
    {
        var mlContext = new MLContext(seed: 42);

        IDataView data = mlContext.Data.LoadFromTextFile<TicketData>(
            dataPath, hasHeader: true, separatorChar: ',', allowQuoting: true);

        var split = mlContext.Data.TrainTestSplit(data, testFraction: 0.2, seed: 42);

        var pipeline = mlContext.Transforms.CustomMapping<TicketData, CombinedText>(
                (input, output) => output.Text = $"{input.Subject} {input.Description}", contractName: null)
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("TextFeatures", nameof(CombinedText.Text)))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", "TextFeatures", nameof(TicketData.Priority)))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapValueToKey("Label", nameof(TicketData.Category)))
            .Append(mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Trainers.SdcaMaximumEntropy("Label", "Features"))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapKeyToValue("PredictedLabel"));

        var model = pipeline.Fit(split.TrainSet);

        var predictions = model.Transform(split.TestSet);
        var metrics = mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Evaluate(predictions, labelColumnName: "Label");

        Console.WriteLine("=== Model Evaluation Metrics ===");
        Console.WriteLine($"MacroAccuracy:  {metrics.MacroAccuracy:F4}");
        Console.WriteLine($"MicroAccuracy:  {metrics.MicroAccuracy:F4}");
        Console.WriteLine($"LogLoss:        {metrics.LogLoss:F4}");
        Console.WriteLine($"LogLossReduction: {metrics.LogLossReduction:F4}");
        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("Per-class metrics:");
        Console.WriteLine($"{"Class",-15} {"Precision",10} {"Recall",10} {"F1Score",10}");
        for (int i = 0; i < metrics.PerClassLogLoss.Count; i++)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Class {i,-10} {metrics.PerClassLogLoss[i],10:F4}");
        }
        Console.WriteLine("================================");

        mlContext.Model.Save(model, data.Schema, modelPath);
        Console.WriteLine($"Model saved to {modelPath}");
    }
}

public class CombinedText
{
    public string Text { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
```

### 4. Create `Controllers/TicketController.cs`
```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;

namespace TicketClassifier.Controllers;

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class TicketController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly PredictionEnginePool<TicketData, TicketPrediction> _predictionEnginePool;

    public TicketController(PredictionEnginePool<TicketData, TicketPrediction> predictionEnginePool)
    {
        _predictionEnginePool = predictionEnginePool;
    }

    [HttpPost("classify")]
    public ActionResult<ClassifyResponse> Classify([FromBody] ClassifyRequest request)
    {
        var input = new TicketData
        {
            Subject = request.Subject,
            Description = request.Description,
            Priority = request.Priority
        };

        var prediction = _predictionEnginePool.Predict(input);

        return Ok(new ClassifyResponse
        {
            PredictedCategory = prediction.PredictedCategory,
            Confidence = prediction.Score.Max()
        });
    }
}

public class ClassifyRequest
{
    public string Subject { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Description { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public int Priority { get; set; }
}

public class ClassifyResponse
{
    public string PredictedCategory { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public float Confidence { get; set; }
}
```

### 5. Update `Program.cs`
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;
using TicketClassifier.Services;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddControllers();

var dataPath = Path.Combine(builder.Environment.ContentRootPath, "Data", "tickets.csv");
var modelPath = Path.Combine(builder.Environment.ContentRootPath, "Data", "model.zip");

// Train model on startup
ModelTrainerService.TrainAndSaveModel(dataPath, modelPath);

// Register pooled prediction engine (thread-safe)
builder.Services.AddPredictionEnginePool<TicketData, TicketPrediction>()
    .FromFile(modelPath);

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
```

### 6. Create directories and files
Create directories `Models`, `Services`, `Controllers` under the project root if they don't exist.

### Important implementation notes
- Use `PredictionEnginePool` (not raw `PredictionEngine`) for thread safety in ASP.NET Core
- The `CustomMapping` approach for combining Subject+Description may have issues with ML.NET's `CustomMapping` in minimal scenarios. If so, consider using a simpler approach: featurize Subject and Description separately, then concatenate all features.
- Actually, `CustomMapping` requires the `[CustomMappingFactoryAttribute]` in some versions. Let's use a simpler approach instead:

**Revised pipeline approach** (avoid CustomMapping complexity):
```csharp
var pipeline = mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("SubjectFeatures", nameof(TicketData.Subject))
    .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("DescriptionFeatures", nameof(TicketData.Description)))
    .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", "SubjectFeatures", "DescriptionFeatures", nameof(TicketData.Priority)))
    .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapValueToKey("Label", nameof(TicketData.Category)))
    .Append(mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Trainers.SdcaMaximumEntropy("Label", "Features"))
    .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapKeyToValue("PredictedLabel"));
```

This is simpler and avoids `CustomMapping` issues. Use this approach.

